// ============================================================================
== 6.1 The Anatomy of the Paper

Sixty questions, four options, no negative marking, and a score out of one hundred
whatever the questions are worth. The paper arrives in a mixed order on the screen; the
syllabus divides it into eight blocks.

#diagram-fig("diagrams/ch06_anatomy.svg",
  "The sixty questions by block. The reading text is ten of them; the comprehension and cloze passages are fifteen more.", width: 100%)

#concept-box("The blocks, and what each one rewards")[
  *Comprehension (5).* Reading the questions first, then the passage.

  *Cloze (10).* Register and collocation — the word that fits the paragraph, not the word that fits the sentence.

  *The reading text (10).* The novel: fact, sequence, character, theme.

  *Sentence interpretation (5).* What a sentence is doing: conceding, contradicting, implying.

  *Antonyms and synonyms (10).* Meaning in context, not the dictionary's first sense.

  *Sentence completion and grammar (10).* Concord, tense, prepositions and connectors.

  *Oral forms (10).* Vowels, consonants, rhyme, word stress and emphatic stress.
]

#rule-box("Three numbers worth knowing")[
  + *60 questions.* Forty seconds each if you spend forty minutes, which leaves five for the review.
  + *100 marks.* The paper is scored out of a hundred however many raw questions you answer, so no single question is worth more than another.
  + *25 per cent.* A blind guess is worth a quarter of a mark. Sixty blanks cost fifteen marks; sixty guesses cost nothing.
]

// ============================================================================
== 6.2 Forty Minutes, Three Passes

#diagram-fig("diagrams/ch06_timing.svg",
  "Three passes over the paper. The first banks what you know, the second works the flags, the third buys back the losses.", width: 100%)

#concept-box("Why three passes beat one long march")[
  A single pass through the paper in order means every hard question costs you two minutes and every easy question at the back of the paper gets whatever time is left — which is usually none. Three passes invert that: the easy questions are banked while the clock is friendly, and the hard ones are answered with the time they actually need.

  The rule that makes it work is simple: *no question gets more than forty seconds on its first sight.*
]

#trap-box("The comprehension trap")[
  The first passage on the screen looks like the most important thing in the paper, because it is the first and it is long. It is five questions. A candidate who spends twelve minutes on it has spent the time of eighteen questions on five.

  Read the questions first, then the passage; mark the sentences the questions point at; answer; leave. Five questions in five minutes.
]

// ============================================================================
== 6.3 The Order of Attack

#diagram-fig("diagrams/ch06_order.svg",
  "The order of attack. The paper does not have to be answered in the order it arrives.", width: 100%)

#rule-box("The order, and the reason for it")[
  + *The reading text first.* Ten questions from a closed text, answered in about four minutes, and entirely inside your own head.
  + *Oral forms second.* Ten questions, mostly one-word decisions, while the mind is fresh.
  + *Comprehension third.* Five questions on one passage, read questions-first.
  + *Cloze fourth.* Ten questions on one short paragraph; read the whole paragraph before filling the first blank.
  + *The lexis blocks last.* Thirty questions of single-word judgement at about thirty seconds each.
]

#shortcut-box("Mark and return is a strategy, not a confession")[
  The CBT engine lets you flag a question and come back. Use it without guilt. A flagged question is not a failure; it is a decision that the next forty seconds are better spent elsewhere.
]

// ============================================================================
== 6.4 Elimination in Every Section

#diagram-fig("diagrams/ch06_elimination.svg",
  "Four eliminations that work in every block of the paper, from the cloze to the oral forms.", width: 100%)

#rule-box("Four eliminations, four examples")[
  + *The category error.* A detail offered as a main idea; a definition offered as a use-in-context; a consonant question answered with a vowel.
  + *The absolute.* *Always*, *never*, *only*, *entirely* — one counter-example kills the option, and the paper usually supplies the counter-example in the passage.
  + *The wrong register.* Grammatical and wrong: a colloquial option in a formal passage, or a formal option in a dialogue.
  + *The echo.* A word repeated from the passage with no idea attached. It looks like evidence and it is decoration.
]

#worked-example(
  1,
  "The arithmetic of the clock",
  [A candidate has forty minutes for sixty questions. The average time available for each question is about

    A. thirty seconds \
    B. forty seconds \
    C. one minute \
    D. ninety seconds],
  [
    *Step 1 — divide.* Forty minutes is 2,400 seconds; 2,400 divided by 60 is 40.

    *Step 2 — allow for the review.* Five minutes of the forty are for the final check, so the working rate is about thirty-five seconds a question.

    *Step 3 — the consequence.* Thirty seconds is not enough to read a passage twice. It is enough to read a stem and eliminate two options, which is why elimination is a timing tool and not a trick.

    *Check.* Forty seconds is the budget; thirty-five is the working rate. The answer is *B*.
  ],
  tier: "FOUNDATION",
)

#worked-example(
  2,
  "The cloze paragraph",
  [“The headmaster arrived at the assembly in his usual \_\_\_\_, a dark suit and a tie that had seen better years.” The best word for the blank is

    A. habit \
    B. attire \
    C. posture \
    D. custom],
  [
    *Step 1 — read for the category.* The blank is followed by a description of clothes, so the missing word must mean *clothing*.

    *Step 2 — test the four.* *Attire* means clothing and fits the register. *Habit* can mean a monk's robe but not a headmaster's suit; *posture* is about the body's position; *custom* is a practice, not a garment.

    *Step 3 — the register check.* A cloze tests the word that fits the paragraph, and *attire* is the formal word a narrator would use in a formal description.

    *Check.* Name the category before you read the options; three of the four are eliminated by category alone. The answer is *B*.
  ],
  tier: "FOUNDATION",
)

#worked-example(
  3,
  "The comprehension order",
  [The most efficient order for a five-question comprehension passage is to

    A. read the passage twice, then the questions \
    B. read the questions, then the passage, then the sentences the questions point at \
    C. read the passage once and answer from memory \
    D. read the options first and guess from them],
  [
    *Step 1 — what the questions are for.* They tell you what the passage is being read for. Reading without them is reading for nothing in particular.

    *Step 2 — the move.* Questions first, passage second, then back to the marked sentences for the answer.

    *Step 3 — the near misses.* A is the expensive habit; C fails on detail questions; D skips the passage, which works only when the options repeat it, and the paper is built to punish that.

    *Check.* The passage is evidence, and evidence is read for a question. The answer is *B*.
  ],
  tier: "UTME STANDARD",
)

#worked-example(
  4,
  "The oral question that turns on one word",
  [“The headmaster LIVES in the school premises.” The word emphasised to correct someone who said he merely visits the school is

    A. headmaster \
    B. lives \
    C. school \
    D. premises],
  [
    *Step 1 — the correction.* The other speaker said he visits. The correction is about *living*, so the emphasis falls on the verb.

    *Step 2 — the test.* Say the sentence with each word stressed and ask which correction it makes. *HEADMASTER* corrects the person; *SCHOOL* corrects the place; *PREMISES* corrects the part of the building. Only *LIVES* corrects the claim.

    *Step 3 — the pattern.* Emphatic-stress questions always supply the false statement in the stem. Find the word that is being contradicted.

    *Check.* The stressed word is the one the other speaker got wrong. The answer is *B*.
  ],
  tier: "UTME STANDARD",
)

#worked-example(
  5,
  "Four minutes left",
  [With four minutes left, twelve questions unanswered and the clock running, the best course is to

    A. work carefully through the twelve in order \
    B. answer all twelve quickly, eliminating where possible, and leave none blank \
    C. leave them blank and review the answered ones \
    D. return to the comprehension passage and re-read it],
  [
    *Step 1 — the arithmetic.* Four minutes for twelve questions is twenty seconds each. Careful work is not available.

    *Step 2 — the value of a guess.* A blank scores zero; a guess scores a quarter of a mark on average. Twelve blanks are worth three marks in expectation, and the elimination you can still do raises that.

    *Step 3 — the near misses.* A spends the time on two questions and abandons ten; C gives away three marks; D spends four minutes on five questions already answered.

    *Check.* In the last minutes, the only wrong move is a blank. The answer is *B*.
  ],
  tier: "JAMB CHALLENGE",
)

// ============================================================================
== 6.5 The Last Five Minutes

#diagram-fig("diagrams/ch06_review.svg",
  "The five-minute review, in order. Blanks first, then negatives, then mis-reads, then transfer.", width: 100%)

#rule-box("The review, in four checks")[
  + *Blanks.* Any empty box gets an answer, however rough.
  + *Negatives.* For every question containing EXCEPT, NOT, LEAST or FALSE, check that you answered the negative and not the positive.
  + *Mis-reads.* Skim the options you chose in the last ten minutes; the eye skips *not* when it is hurrying.
  + *Transfer.* Confirm that every answer has been registered by the engine. A correct answer that was never submitted is a blank.
]

#concept-box("What to do on the day, in one paragraph")[
  Arrive early enough to sit down and breathe. Read the instructions on the screen even though you know them. Start with the reading text — ten questions, four minutes, from a book you have already read. Then the oral forms, then the comprehension passage with the questions first, then the cloze, then the lexis blocks. Forty seconds a question, flag anything that will not come, and never leave a box empty. In the last five minutes, check the blanks, the negatives, the answers you hurried and the transfer. Then stop.
]

